PART 7: CSS CASCADE AND SPECIFICITY
================================================================================

CSS CASCADE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When multiple declarations affect the same CSS property, the browser
determines the winning declaration using the cascade.

Important factors include:

• Origin and importance

• Cascade layers, when used

• Specificity

• Scoping proximity where applicable in modern CSS

• Source order


For basic React/CSS learning, remember:

1. Higher-priority cascade rules win

2. Specificity matters when competing declarations are otherwise comparable

3. When specificity is equal, later applicable declarations generally win


================================================================================
SPECIFICITY - BEGINNER MENTAL MODEL
================================================================================

A useful simplified model:

+-------------------+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| SELECTOR TYPE     | RELATIVE WEIGHT      | EXAMPLE                     |
+-------------------+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Type/Element      | Lowest               | p                           |
| Class             | Higher               | .card                       |
| ID                | Higher               | #main-card                  |
| Inline Style      | Very High            | style={{...}}               |
+-------------------+----------------------+-----------------------------+


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Specificity is more accurately treated as a tuple/category comparison,
not as a literal universal "1 / 10 / 100 / 1000 point system."

The points model is useful only as a beginner shortcut.


================================================================================
SPECIFICITY EXAMPLE 1
================================================================================

p {
  color: blue;
}


.card-title {
  color: red;
}


If the paragraph also has class="card-title", the class selector has
greater specificity for the color declaration.


================================================================================
SPECIFICITY EXAMPLE 2
================================================================================

div.card {
  background: white;
}


.card-container .card {
  background: lightgray;
}


The second selector has greater class specificity.


================================================================================
SPECIFICITY EXAMPLE 3
================================================================================

.card {
  color: blue;
}


#main-card {
  color: red;
}


The ID selector has greater specificity.


================================================================================
CASCADE / SPECIFICITY SUMMARY
================================================================================

+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| SCENARIO             | GENERAL RESULT                                |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| Element vs Class     | Class is more specific                        |
| Class vs ID          | ID is more specific                           |
| Same specificity     | Later applicable declaration usually wins     |
| Inline vs Class      | Inline style normally has priority            |
| !important           | Changes cascade importance                    |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


IMPORTANT ABOUT !important
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do NOT think of !important simply as:

"Overrides absolutely everything."

It participates in the cascade's importance rules.

Practical lesson:

Avoid unnecessary use of !important because it makes CSS harder to override,
reason about, and maintain.


