PART 9: CSS BEST PRACTICES IN REACT
================================================================================

CSS BEST PRACTICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Organize Files Logically
  - Co-locate component CSS when useful

• Use CSS Variables
  - Centralize frequently reused design tokens

• Keep Specificity Manageable
  - Prefer classes over IDs for component styling

• Use Semantic Class Names
  - Describe purpose rather than appearance

• Group Related Rules
  - Keep component rules together

• Maintain Consistent Spacing
  - Reuse spacing variables

• Design Responsively
  - Design from small screens upward when appropriate

• Remove Unused Styles
  - Keep CSS focused

• Document Complex Rules
  - Explain non-obvious selectors

• Avoid Deep Selector Chains
  - Keep rules understandable

• Add Visible Focus States
  - Important for keyboard accessibility

• Respect Reduced Motion Where Appropriate
  - Animations should not become an accessibility barrier


================================================================================
GOOD CSS EXAMPLE
================================================================================

:root {

  --card-bg: white;

  --card-shadow:
    0 4px 8px
    rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);

  --card-radius: 8px;

  --card-padding: 16px;

}


.card {
  background:
    var(--card-bg);

  border-radius:
    var(--card-radius);

  box-shadow:
    var(--card-shadow);

  padding:
    var(--card-padding);

  transition:
    all 0.3s ease;
}


.card:hover {
  transform:
    translateY(-4px);

  box-shadow:
    0 8px 16px
    rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
}


.card__title {
  font-size: 1.25rem;

  font-weight: 600;

  margin-bottom: 8px;
}


.card__description {
  font-size: 0.95rem;

  color: #666;

  line-height: 1.6;
}


.card__button {
  background: #667eea;

  color: white;

  border: none;

  padding:
    8px 16px;

  border-radius: 4px;

  cursor: pointer;

  transition:
    background 0.3s ease;
}


.card__button:hover {
  background: #5568d3;
}


@media (max-width: 768px) {

  .card {
    padding: 12px;
  }


  .card__title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

}


================================================================================
LESS MAINTAINABLE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

.card {
  background: white;
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 16px;
}


.card h2 {
  font-size: 1.25rem;
}


.card p {
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: #666;
}


#card-button {
  background: #667eea;
}


.card button {
  background:
    #667eea !important;
}


Problems:

• ID selector increases specificity

• !important complicates overriding

• Element selectors tightly couple CSS to markup structure

• Shared values aren't centralized


================================================================================
BEST PRACTICES COMPARISON
================================================================================

+----------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------+
| PRACTICE             | LESS MAINTAINABLE         | PREFERRED                   |
+----------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------+
| Naming               | .box                      | .product-card               |
| Specificity          | #card-button              | .card__button               |
| !important           | Routine usage             | Avoid unless justified      |
| Inline styles        | All static styles inline  | Class for static styles     |
| CSS Variables        | Repeated hardcoded values | var(--primary-color)        |
| Organization         | Random rule order         | Component/grouped styles    |
| Comments             | None for complex CSS      | Explain non-obvious rules   |
| Responsive           | Fixed-only layout         | Flexible + media queries    |
| Duplication          | Repeated values           | Shared variables/patterns   |
+----------------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------+


IMPORTANT ABOUT INLINE STYLES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Inline style is not automatically bad in React.

Inline styles can be useful for values that are genuinely dynamic.

Example:

<div
  style={{
    borderColor: color
  }}
/>


For mostly static visual rules, CSS classes are generally easier to manage.


